  "title": "The Indiana Pacers Lost a Season. Did They Lose Their Moment?",
  "subtitle": null,
  "byline": null,
  "publishedAt": null,
  "contentHtml": "<figure><a href=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/%24s_!0xDe!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F32560625-aef7-4926-ba22-499a646f24a6_1900x1267.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\n<img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/%24s_!0xDe!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F32560625-aef7-4926-ba22-499a646f24a6_1900x1267.png\" width=\"1456\" height=\"971\" data-size=\"normal\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\">\n\n\n</a><figcaption><strong> Tyrese Haliburton of the Indiana Pacers looks on during the first half against the Minnesota Timberwolves at Hilton Coliseum during a preseason game on October 07, 2026 in Ames, Iowa.</strong> (Tyler Clouse/Getty Images)</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Ahead of the <a href=\"https://www.espn.com/nba/scoreboard/_/date/20261020\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">2026-27 NBA season tip-off</a> on Tuesday, I’ve spent a lot of time focusing on the <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/neither-won-the-nba-title-now-theyre\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">two-team battle of juggernauts</a> atop the <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/i/216453179/2026-27-nba-team-aggregated-market-forecast\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">championship odds</a> — Oklahoma City versus San Antonio — and the New York Knicks’ status as one of the <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/the-market-still-doesnt-know-what\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">most disrespected defending champions ever</a>. I’ve also spilled ink on the Philadelphia 76ers’ <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/do-the-lebron-sixers-have-a-fit-problem\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">audacious roster experiment</a> with LeBron James joining Jaylen Brown (himself an All-NBA star <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/the-celtics-won-the-future-and-lost\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">controversially shipped to a division rival</a> this summer) — plus Tyrese Maxey, VJ Edgecombe and Joel Embiid — on a <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/lebron-james-just-took-the-biggest\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">historic pay-cut</a>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/subscribe?\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><span>Subscribe now</span></a></p>\n<p>But the most fascinating team of all — and maybe the biggest playoff wild-card, too — could be the Indiana Pacers. When last we saw the Pacers, they were putting the finishing touches on a <a href=\"https://www.basketball-reference.com/teams/IND/2026.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">19-win regular season</a> that “surpassed” 1982-83 as the <a href=\"https://www.basketball-reference.com/teams/IND/#IND::5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">worst in franchise history</a>. But that’s not <em>really </em>when we last saw the Pacers — at least, not according to the narrative they’re hoping to sell us on as they enter 2026-27.</p>\n<p>\n          <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/the-indiana-pacers-lost-a-season\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\n              Read more\n          </a>\n      </p>",
